    "definition": "Mean household income: the total income of all households divided by the number of households. Household income is the past-12-month income of the householder and every other household member aged 15 or older, related or not, from all reported sources such as wages, self-employment, interest, dividends, rental income, Social Security, retirement income and public assistance. Dollar values are adjusted for inflation to 2024 dollars.",
    "notes": "Estimates are survey-based. Margins of error (variable S1901_C01_013M) are at the 90% confidence level; the true value is likely within the estimate plus or minus the margin. Mean is not median: half of Texas households had incomes below the statewide median of $78,476 (S1901_C01_012E), while the mean is pulled higher by a smaller number of very high incomes. Per-capita income is different again: it divides aggregate income by every resident, including children, rather than by households. Each 5-year estimate pools survey responses collected from 2020 through 2024; it describes that period, not a single year, and should not be compared directly with 1-year estimates. Every Texas county has a published mean in this release; a county without one would be shown as not available, never as $0. Small counties carry wide margins; the widest is Kenedy County ($64,860 ± $34,160, ±53%)."
